        "---\n",
        "title: 'Bias in Error Estimation When Using CV'\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Bias in error estimation when using cross-validation for model selection by Varma and Simon (2006)\n",
        "\n",
        "### Paper Summary\n",
        "\n",
        "The goal of the Varma and Simon (2006) paper was to show that if feature selection and classifier parameter tuning are done outside of the cross-validation (CV) loop, it can lead to biased error estimates. The authors also proposed a nested CV approach that was used to develop an unbiased error estimate. \n",
        "\n",
        "### Potential Bias in Error Estimation During CV\n",
        "\n",
        "CV has been shown to be unbiased if all aspects of model training are done inside the CV loop, including feature selection, classifier type selection, and classifier parameter tuning. However, if this process is violated, it leads to biased error estimates. For example, sometimes feature selection is performed on the entire dataset instead of the smaller training set within each CV loop, leading to bias error estimates. Similar issues can occur with classifier parameter tuning. \n",
        "\n",
        "### Nested CV As a Potential Solution\n",
        "\n",
        "The authors used a nested CV method that was used to develop a more unbiased error estimate. The inner CV loop was used to perform hyperparameter tuning, while the outer CV loop was used to determine the error estimate. The authors found that the nested CV approach able to provide a more unbiased error estimate than the standard CV approach.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Methods Used\n",
        "\n",
        "The authors used a simulation study for their analysis.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Study Details\n",
        "\n",
        "The analysis in the paper used 1000 \"null\" simulated datasets consisting of 40 samples and 6000 features for classifier training using both shrunken centroids and support vector machine (SVM) classifiers. The null datasets included a 50/50 split of Class 1 and Class 2. The authors found that when feature selection and classifier hyperparameter tuning were done outside of the CV loop, CV can produce biased error estimates. Although 10-fold CV and leave-one-out CV (LOOCV) resampling methods were evaluated in this study, the result was generalized to be present in all CV methods.\n",
        "\n",
        "The authors also presented a nested CV method that was used to develop a more unbiased error estimate. This method was evaluated using the same simulated datasets and used both shrunken centroids and SVM classifiers. In the nested CV method, the outer CV loop used a LOOCV approach, while the inner CV loop used a 10-fold CV approach to determine the classifier parameters and model. Once the inner CV loop was completed, the model was evaluated on the left out sample from the outer CV loop. The average error from the outer CV loop was shown to be effective in reducing model error bias over the simulated datasets.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Computation Plan\n",
        "\n",
        "My computation plan is to replicate a portion of the simulation performed in the paper. I plan to focus on the shrunken centroids classifier and will use both the standard 10-fold CV approach and the nested CV approach to compare the error estimates. I will consider a smaller number of features (e.g., 100) and a smaller number of simulations (e.g., 20) to reduce computation time. I plan to generate similar histograms to those shown in the paper to illustrate the difference in error estimates between the two CV approaches.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Computation Details\n",
        "\n",
        "First, a simulated \"null\" dataset with 40 samples and 100 features was generated, with a 50/50 split of Class 1 and Class 2."
      ],
